  "meta": {
    "title": "Slick City: Marine Oil-Snow Sinks After a Spill",
    "date": "2026-09-07",
    "lesson": "When crude oil disperses into the ocean, much of it doesn't stay at the surface - it aggregates with mucus, diatom exudate and clay into 'marine oil snow' that sinks through the water column, feeding a fast-changing bacterial succession as it falls. Alkane-degrading specialists (Alcanivorax-type lipid consumers) bloom first on the fresh, lipid-rich flocs near the surface; as the material ages and sinks into cooler, dimmer water, protein and chitin-degrading generalists take over deeper in the column. This level rewards chemotaxis toward lipid-rich particles near the surface and enzyme flexibility as the same falling floc gets tougher to digest with depth and cold.",
    "citation": "Passow & Ziervogel (2016), Deepwater Horizon marine oil snow sinking and MOSSFA (Marine Oil Snow Sedimentation and Flocculent Accumulation) event studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "2010 Deepwater Horizon spill, northern Gulf of Mexico - marine oil snow sedimentation and flocculent accumulation (MOSSFA) event"
  },
